### What does Collectors.toMap do?

Collectors.toMap(keyMapper, valueMapper) iterates the stream and applies two functions to each element: one to produce the map key and one to produce the map value. The result is a Map where each element contributes one entry.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class IdToNameMap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
            new Employee(1001L, "Rajesh", "Loans"),
            new Employee(1002L, "Priya",  "IT"),
            new Employee(1003L, "Amit",   "Finance")
        );

        Map<Long, String> idToName = employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                Employee::getId,
                Employee::getName));

        System.out.println(idToName);
        System.out.println("ID 1002: " + idToName.get(1002L));
    }
}
```

```text
{1001=Rajesh, 1002=Priya, 1003=Amit}
ID 1002: Priya
```

### What happens when two elements produce the same key?

The two-argument toMap throws an IllegalStateException because it does not know which value to keep. This is actually a useful safety net: it surfaces a data integrity problem rather than silently discarding a record.

```java
List<Employee> withDuplicate = List.of(
    new Employee(1001L, "Rajesh", "Loans"),
    new Employee(1001L, "Raj",    "IT")     // same id!
);

// WRONG: throws IllegalStateException
Map<Long, String> crash = withDuplicate.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName));
// Exception: Duplicate key 1001 (attempted merging Rajesh and Raj)
```

### How do you handle duplicate keys?

Use the three-argument form with a merge function that decides which value to keep.

```java
// Keep the first value encountered
Map<Long, String> keepFirst = withDuplicate.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName,
        (existing, incoming) -> existing));

// Keep the last value encountered
Map<Long, String> keepLast = withDuplicate.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName,
        (existing, incoming) -> incoming));

// Concatenate both
Map<Long, String> concat = withDuplicate.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName,
        (a, b) -> a + ", " + b));
System.out.println(concat); // {1001=Rajesh, Raj}
```

### How do you control the map type?

The default is HashMap (unordered). Use the four-argument form to specify a different map implementation.

```java
// Sorted by id using TreeMap
Map<Long, String> sorted = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName,
        (a, b) -> a,
        TreeMap::new));

// Insertion-order using LinkedHashMap
Map<Long, String> ordered = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName,
        (a, b) -> a,
        LinkedHashMap::new));
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: null values cause NullPointerException.

```java
// WRONG: null name throws NPE
List<Employee> withNull = List.of(
    new Employee(1001L, null, "Loans")
);
withNull.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        Employee::getName));  // NullPointerException!

// CORRECT: map null to a sentinel
withNull.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        e -> e.getName() != null ? e.getName() : "UNKNOWN"));
```

Mistake 2: expecting ordered output from HashMap.

```java
// The iteration order of HashMap is unpredictable.
// Use TreeMap for sorted keys or LinkedHashMap for insertion order.
```

### How is toMap different from groupingBy?

| Feature | toMap | groupingBy |
|---|---|---|
| Value per key | One value | Collection (List, Set, etc.) |
| Duplicate keys | Throws or merge | Collects into group |
| Use case | Unique keys lookup | One-to-many grouping |
| Default map | HashMap | HashMap |

Use toMap when each key maps to exactly one value. Use groupingBy when multiple elements share a key.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- toMap internally uses Map.merge(), which is why null values throw NPE.
- For an id-keyed lookup, uniqueness is the contract. The two-argument form enforces this and is the correct, intention-revealing choice.
- In parallel streams, toMap uses a concurrent merge strategy. Supply a ConcurrentHashMap supplier for best parallel performance.
- When building a lookup that is queried frequently, the O(1) HashMap lookup amortises the O(n) collection cost.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you build a Map<String, Employee> keyed by name?" Same pattern: toMap(Employee::getName, Function.identity()).
- "How do you build an unmodifiable map?" Wrap with Collections.unmodifiableMap() or use Collectors.toUnmodifiableMap() (Java 10+).
- "How do you handle null keys?" toMap does not reject null keys, but HashMap allows only one null key.
